Walk the string from the last index down to 0 and append each character to the result (or swap characters from both ends of a character array). O(n) time, O(n) space. Note: prepending (rev = ch + rev) is also correct but O(n^2) in languages with immutable strings — acceptable for this size, mention it if discussing complexity.

def reverseString(s):
    out = []
    for i in range(len(s) - 1, -1, -1):
        out.append(s[i])
    return ''.join(out)

One pass: lowercase each character; if it is an English letter, increment vowels when it is in 'aeiou', otherwise consonants. Non-letters are skipped. O(n) time, O(1) space.

def countVowelsConsonants(s):
    vowels = consonants = 0
    for ch in s.lower():
        if 'a' <= ch <= 'z':
            if ch in 'aeiou':
                vowels += 1
            else:
                consonants += 1
    return [vowels, consonants]

Scan once, counting the positions where a non-whitespace character starts a new word (the previous character was whitespace or there was none). Splitting on runs of whitespace and counting the non-empty parts is also fine. O(n) time, O(1) space.

def countWords(s):
    count = 0
    in_word = False
    for ch in s:
        if ch.isspace():
            in_word = False
        elif not in_word:
            count += 1
            in_word = True
    return count

